# Classes and Objects

A boba cafe near campus wants to know if it can survive the lunch rush, so we're going to write a simulator for it. It's the same one the lecture builds.

![a glass of pearl milk tea with dark tapioca pearls at the bottom](courses/diagrams/e1e9c215-66b6-55eb-8d65-fc8d064efe01/bubble-tea.jpg)
*Image: Battlesnake1, CC0, via Wikimedia Commons.*

One order visits four stations. A `Drink` gets passed along, and each station fills in one part of it.

![a drink passes through the kiosk, brewer, cooker and topping station](courses/diagrams/e1e9c215-66b6-55eb-8d65-fc8d064efe01/flow.svg)

You could write that as one long function. That's **procedural** programming: break the problem into steps (C works this way). **Functional** programming breaks it into functions with inputs, outputs and no side effects. **Object-oriented** programming breaks it into **objects**, each holding data plus the functions that work on that data. C++ supports OOP but doesn't force it on you.

So each station becomes a **class**, and so does the cafe:

```cpp
class BobaCafe {
    TeaBrewer teaBrewer;          // private: the implementation
    BobaCooker bobaCooker;
    ToppingStation toppingStation;
    OrderKiosk orderKiosk;
    int num_drinks_served;
public:                           // public: the interface
    Drink doOrder(const std::string& customer_name);
};
```

A class is the blueprint. An **object** (or **instance**) is one thing built from it, with its own copy of the member variables.

![one class and two objects built from it](courses/diagrams/e1e9c215-66b6-55eb-8d65-fc8d064efe01/objects.svg)

Code outside the class can only use the public part. Putting data and functions together behind an interface like this is called **encapsulation**. The cafe can swap out its cooker and nobody who calls `doOrder` has to change.

A `struct` is the same thing with one difference: its members (and its inheritance) are **public by default**, while a class starts out private. Use a struct for a plain bag of data like `Drink`, and a class when there's behavior and something to hide.

