# Static and Const Members

The cafe opens a second location. Each one counts its own drinks, but "how many cafes are open?" isn't a fact about any one cafe. It's a fact about the whole class.

That's a **static data member**: one copy, shared by every object. It is **not** stored inside each object. Make ten cafes and there are still exactly one `num_open`, and a change made through any cafe is seen by all of them.

![two cafes with their own ids sharing one static counter](courses/diagrams/d5404960-7481-5543-88b0-94907e1aa0c6/shared.svg)

```cpp
class BobaCafe {
public:
    static int getNumOpen() { return num_open; }     // static member function
    int served() const { return num_drinks_served; } // const member function
private:
    static int num_open;      // declared here
    const int id;             // set in the init list, then never changes
    int num_drinks_served;
};

// bobacafe.cpp
int BobaCafe::num_open = 0;   // defined once, and no 'static' here
```

A **static member function** belongs to the class too. You can call it with no object at all, as `BobaCafe::getNumOpen()`. So it has **no `this`** (the pointer to the object a method was called on), and it can only use static members and other static functions.

A **const member function** promises not to modify its object. It can't change non-static members. It *can* change static ones, since those aren't part of the object.

The word `const` means something different in each spot. Read this left to right:

```cpp
const string& foo(const string& arg) const;
// 1st: the caller can't modify what's returned
// 2nd: foo can't modify arg
// 3rd: foo can't modify *this (member functions only)
```

