# Overloading Operators

The delivery cart needs to track where it is on campus, as a `Vector2D`. It'd be nice to write `position + step` like we would with numbers.

We can, because **operators are just functions with funny names**. `a + b` can be a method called `operator+`:

![a + b becomes a.operator+(b)](courses/diagrams/c7a28683-3593-5e8e-914a-bdeaf0d00eb0/call.svg)

```cpp
class Vector2D {
public:
    Vector2D(double x = 0, double y = 0) : x(x), y(y) {}
    Vector2D operator+(const Vector2D& r) const {
        return Vector2D(x + r.x, y + r.y);
    }
private:
    double x, y;
};
```

Being a member is **one option, not a rule**. Most operators can also be written as a free function outside the class (next lesson), and a few, like `<<` for printing, have to be.

A member operator has **one** parameter, because the left operand is already `*this`. It can read `r.x` even though that's private: privacy is per class, so any object of the same class is fair game.

Notice the two `const`s. Adding shouldn't change either side. It returns a brand new vector.

> [!note]
> The operand types are known when you compile, so picking the operator is **static** polymorphism.

## Assignment

Add three member operators to `Vector2D`.

1. [ ] `operator+`, with one parameter.
2. [ ] `operator-`.
3. [ ] `operator==`: `true` only if **both** x and y match.

It won't compile until all three exist. An error starting `no match for 'operator` names the one that's still missing.

