# Friend Functions

We'd love `cout << position` to work. But the left operand there is the stream, and we can't add methods to the stream's class. So `<<` and `>>` **can't be members** of `Vector2D`. They have to be functions outside it.

An outside function can't see `x` and `y`. A **friend** is an outside function that the class grants access to its private members.

![members and friends reach private data, plain functions use getters](courses/diagrams/55c99635-64f4-5450-a86f-9631b17e60ba/who.svg)

The word `friend` goes on the declaration **inside** the class, and nowhere else:

```cpp
// inside the class
friend Vector2D operator+(const Vector2D& a, const Vector2D& b);
friend std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Vector2D& v);

// outside: no 'friend', no 'Vector2D::'
Vector2D operator+(const Vector2D& a, const Vector2D& b) {
    return Vector2D(a.x + b.x, a.y + b.y);
}
std::ostream& operator<<(std::ostream& os, const Vector2D& v) {
    return os << "(" << v.x << ", " << v.y << ")";   // return os so << chains
}
```

| `operator+` form | Parameters | Private access |
|---|---|---|
| member | 1 (left is `*this`) | yes, both objects' |
| friend (not a member) | **2** | yes |
| plain non-member | **2** | no, use the getters |

`>>` takes a `std::istream&` and a `Vector2D&` that is **not** const, since reading changes it.

One more reason to like non-members, from Lab 1: the constructor can turn a 3 into a vector, so a member `+` allows `v + 3`, but never `3 + v`. A non-member allows both.

## Assignment

1. [ ] Write `operator+` as a friend, so `3 + v` works.
2. [ ] Write a friend `operator<<` that prints `(x, y)`, like `(4, 6)`, and returns the stream.
3. [ ] Write a friend `operator>>` that reads x and then y, like `7 8`, and returns the stream.

It won't compile until all three exist.

