# Member or Free Operator?

After the last two lessons you've seen `operator+` written both ways. Here's the rule set in one place.

| Operator | Member? | Free function (friend or not)? |
|---|---|---|
| `+ - * / == < !=` and most others | allowed | allowed |
| `<<`, `>>` with a stream on the left | not possible (the stream is on the left) | **required** |
| `=`, `[]`, `()`, `->` | **required** | not allowed |

So "an overloaded operator must be a member function" is **false**. A free function is defined outside the class, with no `ClassName::` in front. It can be a **friend** (declared with `friend` inside the class, so it can read private members) or a plain function that only uses the public getters.

```cpp
class Money {
    int cents;
public:
    Money(int c = 0) : cents(c) {}
    int getCents() const { return cents; }
    friend bool operator==(const Money& a, const Money& b);    // friend: may read cents
};

bool operator==(const Money& a, const Money& b) { return a.cents == b.cents; }

Money operator*(const Money& a, int k) {                       // plain free function:
    return Money(a.getCents() * k);                             // only the public getter
}
```

