# Changing an Array in Place

The housing office bills rent per quarter now, not per month. Every room's monthly rent in our array has to become a quarterly rent: three times as much.

Since an array parameter is really a pointer to the caller's first element, a function can change the caller's array directly:

```cpp
void tripleAll(int arr[], int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) arr[i] *= 3;
}

int rent[] = {700, 950, 820};
tripleAll(rent, 3);      // rent is now {2100, 2850, 2460}
```

![the rent array before and after tripling](courses/diagrams/4b9956e2-97b8-5f99-aafa-3e6c8bfe145d/pic.svg)

There's no `&` and no return value. `arr[i] *= 3` follows the pointer and writes into `rent` itself. That's also why the length comes in as its own parameter `n`: the function only has a pointer and can't find out how long the array is.

> [!warning]
> Watch the loop bound. `i < n` touches indexes `0` to `n - 1`. Writing `i < n - 1` misses the last element, and `i <= n` writes one past the end.

## Assignment

1. [ ] Complete `tripleAll(arr, n)` in `rooms.h` so it triples every one of the `n` elements, in place.

