# Shallow Copies

Lab 2's `myArray` (later `arrayList`) holds its data on the heap and remembers the size:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class myArray {
    T* array;
    int allocated_size;
public:
    myArray(int size = 0);
    ~myArray();                      // delete[] array;
    T& operator[](int idx);
    int size();
};
```

The housing office copies a floor's plan so they can draft next semester's changes. Watch what happens:

```cpp
myArray<int> a(50);
a[0] = 1;
myArray<int> b = a;     // copy constructor (b is being created)
b[0] = 2;
if (a[0] == b[0]) cout << "Uh oh";
```

It prints **Uh oh**. We never wrote a copy constructor, so C++ made a default one, and the default copies each member one by one:

```cpp
myArray(const myArray& source)
    : array(source.array),                     // pointer copy only!
      allocated_size(source.allocated_size) {}
```

![a and b both point at the same heap array](courses/diagrams/2d72f1ca-7fe7-566c-905c-d75399421d65/pic.svg)

That's a **shallow copy**: two objects, one array. Changing `b` changes `a`. Worse, at the end of the function both destructors run `delete[]` on the **same** array, which is a double delete.

What we want is a **deep copy**: the new object allocates its own array and copies each element into it.

