# Templates

Our `IntArray` only holds `int`s. The app also needs an array of room names (`std::string`) and an array of `Room` objects. Copying the whole class three times and changing the type would work, until you find a bug and have to fix it three times.

A **template** lets you write the class once, with a placeholder type `T`:

```cpp
template <typename T>
class myArray {
    T* array;
    int allocated_size;
public:
    myArray(int size = 0) : array(new T[size]), allocated_size(size) {}
    ~myArray() { delete[] array; }
    T& operator[](int idx);
    int size();
};
```

When you use it, you say which type `T` is:

```cpp
myArray<int> beds(50);
myArray<std::string> names(50);
```

![the compiler makes myArray of int and myArray of string from one template](courses/diagrams/f6a9f3d3-3525-5a97-b7ae-c7c0c87daada/pic.svg)

The compiler generates a **separate class** for each type you use. `myArray<int>` and `myArray<std::string>` are two different types that happen to come from the same blueprint.

This is a form of **static polymorphism** (the last ones were function and operator overloading). "Static" means the type `T` decides the code at **compile time**:

- **Faster**: no run-time lookup, unlike virtual functions.
- **Less flexible**: the type has to be known when you compile.
- **Code bloat**: every new `T` means another full copy of the class and its methods in your program.

