# Nested Loops

When the inner loop's bound **doesn't depend** on the outer loop's index, the inner loop runs the same number of times on every pass. Then you can simply **multiply**.

The lecture's example, assuming every operation is O(1):

```cpp
// 3 operations
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    // 5 operations
    for (int j = 0; j < n; j++) {
        // 20 operations
    }
}
```

Count: 3 + n · (5 + n · 20) = 20n² + 5n + 3. Drop the lower terms and the constant: **O(n²)**.

Change the inner bound to `j < 20` and the count is 3 + n · (5 + 20 · 20) = 405n + 3, which is **O(n)**. A loop that runs a **fixed** number of times is just a constant.

Exact counts are fair game too. With `j < n/2`, every row of the grid has n/2 cells:

![six by six grid with the first three columns highlighted in every row](courses/diagrams/fdc166f5-578f-5dcb-b171-d6b0cbfcbd8a/half.svg)

That's n · n/2 = **n²/2** runs. Still O(n²), but if a question asks "exactly how many times," the answer is n²/2, not n².

A step of 2 gives the same count: `for (int j = 0; j < n; j += 2)` visits j = 0, 2, 4, …, so it also runs n/2 times per row, n²/2 in total.

> [!warning]
> "Multiply" only works when the inner count is the same every time. When it depends on i, you'll **sum** instead. That's the next lesson.

