# Dependent Inner Loops

When the inner bound uses the outer index, each pass of the outer loop does a different amount of work. You can't multiply. Make a table of i and the inner count, then **add up the column**.

The lecture's example:

```cpp
for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    // 5 operations
    for (int j = 0; j < i * i; j++) {
        // 10 operations
    }
}
```

| i | 0 | 1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | … | n − 1 |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| inner runs | 0 | 1 | 4 | 9 | 16 | … | (n − 1)² |

![bars of length 0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25](courses/diagrams/8d53207e-538d-5d88-a308-be067ad8efff/squares.svg)

The sum 0² + 1² + … + (n − 1)² = (n − 1)n(2n − 1)/6, which is about **n³/3**. So this is **O(n³)**, even though it's only two loops.

Two series to put on your note sheet:

- 1 + 2 + … + n = n(n + 1)/2 (arithmetic)
- 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 2ᵏ = 2ᵏ⁺¹ − 1 (geometric, coming up soon)

