# Logarithmic Loops

Here's binary search from the lecture. `array` is sorted.

```cpp
int b = 0;
int e = array.len();
while (b != e) {
    int m = (b + e) / 2;
    if (array[m] == key) return m;
    else if (array[m] < key) b = m + 1;
    else e = m;
}
```

![binary search for 23 in 16 sorted values: each pass looks at the middle and throws away half](courses/diagrams/026a233e-6cf2-59f9-9f9f-cff3b54024cf/trace.svg)

Run the five steps. (1) The input is the array's length n. (2) Each pass sets b or e so the search space halves: n, n/2, n/4, …, n/2ᵏ. It stops when that reaches 1, so k = log₂ n passes. (3) Each pass does O(1) work. (4) log n passes times O(1). (5) **O(log n)**.

Any loop whose counter is **multiplied or divided** by a constant each time runs a logarithmic number of times: `i *= 2`, `i /= 2`, `i *= 3`. The base doesn't matter for Big-Oh.

Lab 3's `func1` combined that with other loops:

```cpp
void func1(int n) {
    int num_iterations = 0;
    int bound = countHalvings(n);           // O(log n), bound = log n
    for (int i = 0; i < n; i++)
        for (int j = 0; j < bound; j++)
            for (int k = 0; k < bound; k++)
                num_iterations++;
}
```

The loops run n · log n · log n times, so `func1` is **O(n log² n)**. The O(log n) call to `countHalvings` happens once and gets added, then dropped.

