# Counting Recursive Calls

Recursive code has no loop to count, so count **calls**. Ask two questions:

- **How many recursive calls does each call make?** Call it b.
- **How deep does it go?** Call it d.

| b | Total calls | Big-Oh |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | d + 1 | O(d) |
| 2 | 1 + 2 + 4 + … + 2ᵈ | O(2ᵈ) |
| 3 | 1 + 3 + 9 + … + 3ᵈ | O(3ᵈ) |

Then multiply by the work **per call**.

The lecture's `sumArray` makes one call, on `len - 1`:

```cpp
int sumArray(int array[], int len) {
    if (len == 0) return 0;
    return array[len - 1] + sumArray(array, len - 1);
}
```

b = 1, d = n, O(1) per call: **O(n)**. Every call waits on the call stack until the one below it returns, so it also uses **O(n) stack space**.

Now a function that calls itself **twice** on n − 1:

```cpp
void g(int n) {
    if (n == 0) return;
    g(n - 1);
    g(n - 1);
}
```

![call tree of g(3) with 15 calls](courses/diagrams/f94b018d-ada8-5e24-9ad2-21b6ae0b3dc4/calls.svg)

b = 2, d = n, so **O(2ⁿ)**. g(3) makes 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 calls. The midterm justification: "each call makes two recursive calls, and the depth is n because n drops by one each time, so O(2ⁿ)." With three calls it's O(3ⁿ).

