# Recursion That Halves

The depth isn't always n. When each call works on **n/2**, the depth is only log₂ n.

**One call on n/2.** The midterm's fast power function:

```cpp
long power(long x, int n) {
    if (n == 0) return 1;
    long half = power(x, n / 2);
    if (n % 2 == 0) return half * half;
    else return x * half * half;
}
```

b = 1, d = log n, O(1) per call: **O(log n)**. The trick is that it calls itself once and saves the result in `half`.

**Two calls on n/2.**

```cpp
int h(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) return 1;
    return h(n / 2) + h(n / 2);
}
```

![call tree of h(8) with 15 calls](courses/diagrams/f2c9ddad-0607-5b34-b950-27b9a2401d4f/halves.svg)

b = 2 and d = log₂ n, so 2^(log₂ n) = **n** leaves, and about 2n − 1 calls in all. That's **O(n)**, not O(2ⁿ). Writing `power` with `power(x, n/2) * power(x, n/2)` would turn it into this shape and make it O(n).

**When the work per call shrinks, add it up level by level.** Merge sort does n work on each level (the halves together still hold n items) and has log n levels: **O(n log n)**.

