# Middle of a Doubly Linked List

Recursion works on linked lists too. Recursion Example 2: given a doubly linked list's head and tail, find the **middle** element in a single traversal. Assume at least one node.

```cpp
struct DNode {
    int elt;
    DNode *prev, *next;
};
```

The idea: walk in from **both ends at once**. Each call moves `head` forward one node and `tail` back one node, so the list "shrinks" by two from the outside.

```cpp
int findMiddle(DNode* head, DNode* tail) {
    if (head == tail || head == tail->prev) return head->elt;
    return findMiddle(head->next, tail->prev);
}
```

Two base cases, one for each parity:

- `head == tail`: odd length, they met on the middle node.
- `head == tail->prev`: even length, they're neighbors. Return `head`, the **first** of the two middles.

![head and tail step inward on 1, 2, 3, 4](courses/diagrams/b92cd515-eaa4-547e-bda8-dac961294644/steps.svg)

> [!warning]
> The slow/fast pointer trick from the linked list chapter returns the **second** middle on an even list (3 for 1, 2, 3, 4). The professor's `findMiddle` returns the **first** (2). (The lecture's problem statement accepted either middle, but his code returns the first, so trace it that way.)

## Assignment

This starts `lists.h`, which has both node types.

1. [ ] Complete `findMiddle(head, tail)` recursively. On an even-length list, return the **first** middle. You can assume the list has at least one node.

