# Reversing a List Recursively

Printing backward leaves the list alone. Now the club wants the move list actually **reversed**, so the robot can replay it from the end. Recursion Example 3: reverse a singly linked list recursively and return the new head.

The professor's version uses a helper that carries the **previous** node down as `last`:

```cpp
Node* reverseHelper(Node* ptr, Node* last) {
    if (ptr->next == nullptr) {
        ptr->next = last;
        return ptr;           // the old tail is the new head
    }
    Node* newHead = reverseHelper(ptr->next, ptr);
    ptr->next = last;
    return newHead;
}

Node* reverse(Node* ptr) {
    if (ptr == nullptr) return ptr;
    return reverseHelper(ptr, nullptr);
}
```

For 1 → 2 → 3 the calls are `(1, null)`, `(2, 1)`, `(3, 2)`. Each frame remembers who came before it, and points itself back at that node:

![reversing 1, 2, 3 one frame at a time](courses/diagrams/4fd861c1-1ac9-5f64-9e3f-faace5397368/steps.svg)

- **Base case**: the last node. Point it at `last` and return it; it's the new head.
- Every other frame passes `newHead` straight back up unchanged, after pointing itself at `last`.
- The very first call has `last = nullptr`, so the old head becomes the new tail.

> [!note]
> Another common version is `newHead = reverse(head->next); head->next->next = head; head->next = nullptr;`. It works too, but the lecture's version is the helper above, so practice tracing that one.

## Assignment

1. [ ] Complete `reverseHelper(ptr, last)` as described.
2. [ ] Complete `reverse(ptr)`: return `nullptr` for an empty list, otherwise start the helper with `last = nullptr`. Relink the existing nodes; don't use `new`.

