# Queue Wrap-Around

Both indexes only ever move forward, and `% N` sends them around the corner:

```cpp
// enqueue
Q[r] = e;
r = (r + 1) % N;

// dequeue
e = Q[f];
f = (f + 1) % N;
```

Watch an 8-slot print queue go around:

![f and r wrapping past the end of an eight-slot array](courses/diagrams/6d778345-a364-54ad-a4cd-00c0d70cc28d/steps.svg)

In step 3 the queue reads A B C D, even though D is in slot 0. **Queue order starts at f**, never at index 0.

Dequeue doesn't erase the old slot either. It only moves `f` past it (red in the picture).

> [!tip]
> To trace one on paper, draw the N boxes and keep f and r in two columns off to the side. Update exactly one of them per operation. `front()` and `size()` update neither.

