# Coding a Circular Deque

A circular-array deque is our circular queue with two new moves, both **backward**.

![f and r stepping backward around the array](courses/diagrams/468a5e04-b897-5334-80ca-90383ad5df75/steps.svg)

```cpp
// insertFront (not full): step back, then write
f = (f - 1 + N) % N;
Q[f] = e;

// removeBack (not empty): step back, then read
r = (r - 1 + N) % N;
return Q[r];

// last (not empty): look one behind r, moving nothing
return Q[(r - 1 + N) % N];
```

The `+ N` is not optional. A C++ `%` keeps the sign of the left side, so without it you index `Q[-1]`.

> [!note]
> Lab 6 makes this growable, with the same `grow()` you just wrote. Its bonus uses a deque of **indices** to find the max of every sliding window in O(n): each index gets inserted once and removed at most once.

## Assignment

Give the print desk its deque. It has a fixed size, so it throws when full.

1. [ ] `insertFront(v)`: throw `FullDequeException()` when full.
2. [ ] `removeBack()`: remove and return the last job, or throw `EmptyDequeException()`.
