# Merge Sort on a Linked List

Merge sort has one more trick: on a **linked list** it needs no extra array at all. Merging is just relinking nodes, which you did in the Linked Lists chapter. Lab 5 asks for exactly this.

The only new part is finding the middle so you can cut the list in two. You can't jump to index n/2 in a list. Instead, use a slow and a fast pointer: slow moves one node at a time, fast moves two. When fast reaches the end, slow is at the middle.

![slow and fast pointers walking a 7-node list; slow stops on the 4th node and the list is cut after it](courses/diagrams/e49b50db-6770-5455-879e-dd57084100f9/middle.svg)

```cpp
Node* mergeSort(Node* head) {
    if (!head || !head->next) return head;      // 0 or 1 node: sorted
    Node* slow = head;
    Node* fast = head->next;                    // start one ahead
    while (fast && fast->next) {
        slow = slow->next;
        fast = fast->next->next;
    }
    Node* second = slow->next;
    slow->next = nullptr;                       // cut after slow
    return merge(mergeSort(head), mergeSort(second));
}
```

> [!warning]
> Why does `fast` start at `head->next`? On a list of 2 nodes, starting both at `head` would leave slow on the **second** node, so the cut produces a 2-node list and an empty one. The 2-node list recurses forever.

Sorting by relinking, with no extra array, is why the lecture says to choose merge sort when the data is a linked list.

## Assignment

The arcade's live feed is a singly linked list of scores. `merge` from the Linked Lists chapter is already written for you.

1. [ ] Complete `mergeSort(head)`: sort the list ascending and return the new head. Only relink nodes: no `new`, no `delete`, and don't change any `elt`.

