# The BinaryTree Class

On the exam you may be asked to write part of Lab 8's class by hand, so here's how its pieces fit together.

```cpp
template <typename T>
class BinaryTree {
public:
    ~BinaryTree() { destroy(root); }
    int countLeaves() const { return leaves(root); }
    // ...
protected:
    BinaryTreeNode<T>* root;
private:
    int leaves(BinaryTreeNode<T>* t) const;   // the recursive helper
    void destroy(BinaryTreeNode<T>*& t);
};
```

**Public method, private helper.** The public functions take no node: you just call `tree.countLeaves()`. But the recursion needs a node to work on. So each public function calls a private recursive helper on `root`, and the helper does the work. Every function from the last few lessons becomes a helper like this.

**`root` is protected, not private.** The binary search tree class is derived from `BinaryTree`, and it needs to reach `root`.

**`this->root` in a derived template.** Inside `class BinarySearchTree : public BinaryTree<T>`, the compiler won't find the inherited `root` on its own, because it lives in a base class that depends on `T`. Write `this->root`.

**The rule of three.** The class owns nodes on the heap, so it needs a destructor (`destroy(root)`), a deep copy constructor and an assignment operator. Both copies can use `copy()`. Assignment must check for self-assignment first.

**Templates live in headers.** All of `BinaryTree`'s code, including every member function body, goes in `binaryTree.h`.

**Insert and delete aren't here.** A plain binary tree has no rule for where a new node goes, so those can be left virtual. The BST supplies the rule.

